Practise here. This page is yours: move, type, kill and undo as you like.

Compos checkpoints buffers, including unsaved ones, so their text can survive a restart. Put text in a file when it should also exist outside Compos and have a path that other programs can open.

A **file** is data on disk. A **buffer** is a named place where the editor holds text. A file buffer visits the file it came from: editing the buffer changes the buffer, and `⌃X ⌃S` writes the buffer back to the file.

This TUTORIAL buffer does not visit the master file. So your edits cannot change the installed tutorial.

Almost everything you see in Compos lives in a buffer. Files are one kind. Help pages, chats, directory listings, web pages, mail, process output, search results and dashboards are buffers too.

Because of that, they share the same window commands, links, search, modes, saved state, groups and agent context.
